window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][140] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you get an engineer to build something they don't want to build?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I explain why it's important until they agree.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Understands the engineer's concern and finds a shared goal.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the ladder (incentives, shared goal, evidence, pre-wire, clear small ask, credit), trades scope openly, accepts a no that is right, and keeps the relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I start by finding out why they don't want to. It is usually a real reason: risk to the system, maintenance burden, a better use of their time, or distrust that the priority is real. So I ask, 'What worries you about this?' and listen. Then I look for the goal we share: if the feature is to cut failed payments, I show the numbers (3% of payments fail, costing about $40,000 a month) and ask how they'd solve it, since their way may be better than my feature. I offer to trade scope: 'If we cut the report export, does the rest fit in two weeks?' I make the ask small and concrete: 'Can you spend half a day to estimate it and tell me what would make it risky?' If their answer is that it's a bad idea, I take it seriously and ask what evidence would change their mind; sometimes I am wrong. If we still disagree, I say who decides, which is me for what, and they flag the risk in writing. And I give credit: when it ships, I say whose idea made it safer.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Listening; shared goal; evidence with limits; trade of scope; respect; credit.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You need senior leaders to approve a major change. How would you prepare the written proposal, and what goes first?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A slide deck with the main points.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A short document with the ask and recommendation first.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> A narrative decision document with the ask, context, options, a recommendation with reasons, what would change the recommendation, risks and next steps; drafted early, reviewed by outsiders, cut, with numbers and a pre-wired reader list.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a practice Amazon has publicly described, narrative memos in place of slides, and make no claim about any internal process <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon 2017 shareholder letter]</code>. &quot;I'd write a decision document, one to two pages, not slides. The first lines are the decision I need, from whom, by when, and my recommendation, so a reader who stops there knows what to do. Then the context in a handful of sentences with the one number that matters. Then the options, usually three, including doing nothing, each with cost, benefit and risk. Then why I recommend one, and what evidence would change my mind. Then risks and next steps with owners. &quot;I'd allow real time: a good memo takes days, not hours, because writing in full sentences exposes the gaps in my thinking. I'd draft early, ask two people outside the project to read it and tell me the point, cut a third, and check every number against its source. Before the meeting I'd pre-wire the two or three people most likely to object and fold their points into the options. I'd bring the memo to the meeting to be read, not presented, and expect the discussion to start from it.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The ask first; options with costs; humility about what would change the view; real writing effort; pre-wiring.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A senior stakeholder insists on a feature you think will hurt users. You've said no twice. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd keep saying no,&quot; or &quot;I'd just build it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Seeks to understand the goal, proposes evidence and a test, and escalates if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Restates their goal, separates facts from values, agrees criteria in advance, runs the cheapest test, disagrees and commits if overruled, and escalates openly if the harm is serious.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Twice saying no means the conversation isn't working, so I'd change it. First, restate their goal: 'I think you want to raise sign-ups before the quarter ends. Is that right?' and ask what's behind the feature. Often there is a goal I can serve another way. Second, separate what data can settle (does it lower retention?) from what's a judgment (how much do we value short-term sign-ups?). Third, propose a cheap test with criteria agreed in advance: 'Let's try it for 10% of users for two weeks; if retention at day 14 falls by more than a point, we stop.' That turns a fight into an experiment and is hard to refuse. &quot;If they still want it without a test, I would ask who decides. If it is them, I'd say plainly what I think will happen, put my concern and the reversal condition in writing, and commit to doing it well, with monitoring. If I believed it would harm users seriously or break a policy or law, I would raise it with the next level or the relevant owner, openly and telling the stakeholder I am doing so, and I wouldn't build the part I thought was unlawful.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Understanding their goal; a test; commitment; escalation for real harm only.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two senior stakeholders want opposite things from your product, and you can't satisfy both. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd go with the more senior one,&quot; or &quot;I'd find a compromise.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Understands each goal, looks for a shared goal and brings data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Maps both, finds each one's real objective, offers options that serve both goals partly, agrees decision criteria, brings the choice to the common decision-maker with a recommendation, and keeps both informed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd begin by understanding each goal separately and in depth. Suppose the head of sales wants a longer free trial to raise conversion, and the head of finance wants a shorter one to protect cash. I would ask each what they're optimizing, what number they would watch, and what would change their mind. Usually the disagreement is over different time horizons or measures, not over facts. &quot;Then I'd look for a shared goal: 'We both want profitable growth.' I'd turn the question into options with numbers: 7 days, 14 days, or 14 days with a day-7 reminder; and agree with both the criteria beforehand, for example, 'payback within 6 months and conversion up at least a point.' If a cheap test can settle it, I'd run it. If not, I'd write a one-page decision document with the options, my recommendation and the evidence that would change it, and take it to the person who owns the decision above both of them, having shown each stakeholder the document first so neither is surprised. I'd tell them both what was decided and why, and credit what each contributed. I'd avoid picking the more senior person by default, and I'd avoid a compromise that serves neither goal.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Deep understanding of both sides; criteria before the choice; options; no surprises; fairness.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You must decide with about 60% of the data you'd like. How do you communicate your recommendation?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd say I'm not sure and ask for more time.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the recommendation with a confidence level.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the uncertainty statement: what we know, assume, confidence as a number, reversibility, what would change the decision and when it will be checked; time-boxes further data; recommends anyway.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd give a clear recommendation, say how sure I am, and say how we'll find out if I'm wrong. For example: 'Based on a two-week test of 8,000 users, conversion rose 2.1% (interval 0.6% to 3.6%). That is the 60% of the data I'd like: we haven't seen a full month or the mobile app. The interval is above zero, but it leaves out novelty and the mobile app, so I'm about 80% confident the effect holds, and I'd expect it to be between 1% and 3%. The change is reversible within a day. So I recommend shipping to 25% of users now and checking again Friday, with a rule: if conversion falls by more than 1%, we roll back. Waiting for the full month costs us about $20,000 in forgone conversions and would reduce my uncertainty only a little, because the interval is already narrowing.' &quot;I'd say what I assume (that the second half of the month looks like the first) and what would change my mind (a drop on mobile). I'd put a date on the next look. And I would not hide behind the uncertainty: after stating it, I recommend.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A recommendation with calibrated confidence; reversibility; a trigger and a check date; the cost of waiting.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You work with a team about twelve hours away in time. How do you run a project?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd schedule meetings at a time that works for both.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses asynchronous writing, handoffs and limited overlap hours.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Writes for asynchronous reading, protects overlap for discussion and decisions, hands off with a written note, shares the inconvenience of timing, makes decisions and ownership explicit, and checks understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd assume we can't rely on live time, and design for that. Everything that needs a decision or has context goes in a document with the answer, the ask and the deadline in the first paragraph, so that the other side can act while I'm asleep. For overlap, say two hours a day, I'd keep it for what needs live discussion: disagreements, complex tradeoffs and relationship. Everything else goes into writing. &quot;At the end of my day I'd write a handoff: what's done, what's blocked, what's next and what I need by when. They'd do the same for me. For meetings I'd rotate the awkward time so that one side isn't always stretching, with an agenda and notes with decisions and owners afterward. I'd make ownership explicit, since silence at a distance is easy to misread, and I'd check understanding by asking the other side to restate the decision. And I'd invest in the relationship early, with a call to get to know each other, since trust makes asynchronous work easier.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Async-first writing; protected overlap; handoffs; fairness; explicit ownership.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you make sure a message is understood by engineers, designers and legal?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I send the same message to everyone.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tailors the message to each audience's concerns.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Starts from shared facts, gives each audience the version that answers their question, avoids jargon, checks understanding by asking for a restatement, and keeps one source of truth.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I keep one source of truth, a short document with the facts, the decision and the ask, and give each group the framing that answers their question. Engineers want the scope, the constraint and the date: what exactly are we building, what is out, what are the risks? Designers want the user problem, the evidence and the freedom to solve it: what are we trying to change for whom? Legal wants the exposure, the data involved and the markets, and wants it early. I avoid jargon that one group knows and another doesn't, and I define terms once. &quot;I check that the message landed by asking for a restatement ('Can you tell me in your words what we're doing and what you need from me?') or by asking someone outside the team to read the document and say the point. If two groups hear different things, I bring them together with the same one-pager. And I send decisions in writing after a meeting so that memory is not the record.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> One truth, several framings; audience's concerns; checking understanding.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You disagree with a data scientist's interpretation of an experiment. How do you resolve it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd trust the data scientist,&quot; or &quot;I'd argue my view.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks about the method and looks at the data together.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Restates their interpretation, agrees the question and the metric, examines assumptions (sample, duration, novelty, segments), proposes a check that could settle it, and decides who decides and how to proceed in the meantime.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start by making sure I understand their reading and what it rests on: 'Walk me through how you got to this. What does the interval say, and what would make you change your mind?' I often learn something. Then I'd say my concern concretely: 'I'm wondering whether the lift is novelty, since the first week was +4% and the last was +1%.' We'd agree what question the test answers and which metric is primary, because many disagreements are about different questions. &quot;Then I'd look for what would settle it: reading the effect by week, checking segments, extending the test or running a holdout. We'd agree in advance what each result would mean for the decision. If we still disagree, we'd write both readings with the uncertainty and take the decision to the owner, with a recommendation about the next step that is reversible. I'd credit the data scientist's contribution, and if the data shows I was wrong, I'd say so. My aim is the right decision and a working relationship, since I'll need their judgment again.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Understanding the method; a specific concern; agreed criteria; humility; a path to a decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c32-11\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C32-11</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> behavioral · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C32 · <strong>Competency:</strong> BE · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Stripe</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Write the message to executives that a key launch will slip by three weeks because another team's dependency is late.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> A long message that apologizes, blames the other team and leaves the ask unclear.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States the new date, the cause, the impact and the plan.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Facts without blame, a calibrated date with a range, options and a recommendation, what is being done, what is needed and by when, and an offer to discuss; written so that a reader can forward it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I describe a writing style and make no claim about any real company's practice. &gt; <strong>Subject: Launch of the new payouts flow moves from 12 November to 3 December; decision needed by Thursday</strong> &gt; &gt; <strong>Summary.</strong> The launch will slip three weeks, to 3 December. I'm about 70% confident of that date and would plan on 10 December as the date we can stand behind. &gt; &gt; <strong>Cause.</strong> The identity-verification service we depend on will be ready on 24 November, not 3 November. The date was set by the team that owns it; we found out on 28 October when their testing found a defect. &gt; &gt; <strong>Impact.</strong> We miss the start of the holiday campaign; about 15% of planned volume (about $400,000) moves into December. &gt; &gt; <strong>Options.</strong> (A) Ship everything on 3 December. (B) Ship on 12 November without identity verification for returning users (about 80% of volume), adding new users on 3 December. Risk: a split campaign message and a lower verification rate in the interim, which we would cap with limits. &gt; &gt; <strong>Recommendation.</strong> B, if marketing confirms it can run a partial campaign. &gt; &gt; <strong>What I need.</strong> Your decision on A or B by Thursday, so marketing and engineering can plan. &gt; &gt; <strong>What we're doing.</strong> Daily check-ins with the owning team; we've agreed milestones on 12 and 19 November; I'll update you every Tuesday and immediately if the date moves. <em>Why it works.</em> The subject line has the news and the ask. It states facts about the cause without blame (&quot;the date was set by the team that owns it&quot;), gives a range, and offers options with a recommendation and a deadline. It can be forwarded without edits.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Why Apple, and why this team?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Apple makes the best products and I've always wanted to work here.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> A specific product observation, a link to the candidate's experience, and interest in the team's problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Team-specific research from public sources, a critique with an improvement and its cost, craft and ecosystem awareness, honest limits on what the candidate knows, and respect for confidentiality.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>(The candidate has been told the team works on shared photo libraries; the details are invented.)</em> &quot;I've used shared photo libraries with my family for a year, and what I noticed is that the hard part isn't sharing, it's the moment after: a thousand photos arrive and no one knows which ones are meant for them. In my last role I rebuilt a gallery flow and raised weekly active use by 11% by showing fewer, better-chosen items first, so the problem of selection over quantity is one I've worked on. I read what the team has said publicly about the feature and looked at the release notes, and my honest critique is that the 'who is in this photo' suggestion works well for adults and is unreliable for children's changing faces, which I think matters for families; I'd want to understand how the team weighs the privacy of on-device recognition against accuracy. I don't know the constraints, so I'd rather ask than assume. Why now: I want to work where the details of a product are treated as the product, and where privacy is a design decision. I'm curious how the team decides what to leave out.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A specific observation; a link to a past result; a critique with an honest limit; craft and privacy awareness; a question.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you research a company before an interview?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I read the website and look at recent news.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses the product, reads primary sources and forms a view.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Builds a one-page brief in about 90 minutes (products and users, strategy, metrics, competitors, recent moves, my angle, three questions), separates primary sources from inference, and uses it across the loop.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I build a one-page company brief, in about 90 minutes. First I use the product for a week, or at least thirty minutes of real tasks, and write down what I notice. Then I read primary sources: the newsroom, the annual report or investor letter for a public company, the product blog, release notes, help-center pages, and talks by the team's leaders. I write down how the company makes money, its stated priorities and the numbers it reports. I pick two or three competitors and say where the company is strong and weak. I list the last two or three launches or changes. &quot;Then comes the part most people skip: my angle. One observation about the product, one critique with an improvement and its cost, and what I'd examine first in the role. I add three questions that my reading raised. I mark every claim as primary or inference, and I leave out anything I can't source. I use the brief for 'why us,' for product questions, and for the questions at the end.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Method; primary sources; a point of view; honesty about sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A team wants to add a new capability. How do you decide whether it belongs in the operating system or in a separate app?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Put it in the OS because it's more integrated,&quot; or &quot;an app because it's faster.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names criteria such as reach, privacy, discoverability and iteration speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Weighs the criteria with numbers, considers the developer ecosystem and maintenance, proposes a hybrid, names what would change the decision, and considers users with one device and different needs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd decide with criteria, not instinct. The criteria that usually matter: <em>system-wide reach</em> (does the capability need to work inside many other apps?); <em>privacy and trust</em> (does it need system-level access that an app should not have, or processing that should stay on the device?); <em>discoverability</em> (will people find it where they need it?); <em>speed of iteration</em> (apps can update independently; an OS release is slow and carries more risk); and <em>maintenance cost</em> (a system feature must work for years and across devices). &quot;Suppose I weight these 30%, 25%, 15%, 15% and 15%, and score each option from one to five. As an OS feature: reach 5, privacy 4, discoverability 4, iteration 2, maintenance 2: a weighted score of 3.70. As a separate app: 2, 3, 3, 5, 4: 3.15. As a hybrid, a small system layer providing the capability to apps through an interface, with apps shaping the experience: 4, 4, 4, 4, 3: 3.85. The scores are judgments, and the point is to make them visible and challengeable. &quot;So I'd lean to the hybrid: build the system layer only for what must be system-wide (access, privacy, consistency), and let apps, including our own, build experiences on top, so we can iterate faster and let developers extend it. I would also test the choice for a person who owns only one device and for people with different needs, such as accessibility settings: a system layer reaches them by default, while an app reaches only those who find and install it. What would change my mind: evidence that the capability is rarely used inside other apps (then an app is enough), or a privacy requirement that makes anything but a system implementation unsafe (then OS).&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Criteria with weights; a hybrid; ecosystem and privacy thinking; stated reversal conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Design a feature that works better across a person's devices without penalizing people who own only one.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Sync everything between devices.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines a baseline for single-device users and an enhancement for multi-device users.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats the single-device experience as complete, makes the multi-device benefit additive and optional, handles privacy and security of cross-device data, measures both groups, and considers failure and setup.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;Principle: the single-device experience is complete, and the second device makes it better, never the other way round. Take the example of a feature for handing off a task, say continuing a document or a call on another device. The baseline: on one device, the feature works fully, and nothing nags the person to buy another. The enhancement: when a second device is nearby and signed in to the same account, the task offers to continue there, with no setup beyond signing in. &quot;Design details. <em>Discovery:</em> show the enhancement only when it is relevant (a second device is present), never as an advertisement. <em>Privacy:</em> data moves between the person's devices with end-to-end protection, processed on the device where possible, and the person can turn it off per device. <em>Failure:</em> if the second device is off or out of range, the first continues without error. <em>Setup:</em> automatic when the account matches; no pairing codes where avoidable. <em>Accessibility:</em> the handoff works with assistive features on both devices. &quot;Measurement. For multi-device users: the share who use the handoff weekly, time to resume a task and satisfaction. For single-device users, the guardrail: the feature's completion rate and satisfaction must not be lower than before, and they must not report being nagged. Suppose (invented) 60% of active users have two or more devices; the benefit is focused on 60%, and the risk is a worse experience for 40%. I'd test with a holdout, and read the single-device guardrail by week.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A complete baseline; an additive enhancement; privacy and failure design; two-group measurement.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A team wants to log more usage data to improve a feature. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;More data is better for improving the product, so log it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what question the data answers and considers privacy.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies necessity, alternatives (on-device processing, aggregation, sampling), consent, retention and access; does sample-size arithmetic to show how much data is needed; sets a decision rule and a review.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I'm describing principles Apple has said it applies publicly, such as on-device processing and privacy-preserving techniques, and nothing about any real system <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Apple privacy pages]</code>. &quot;I'd start by asking what decision the data will inform. If the team can't state the question and the metric, the answer is no. Then I'd work down a ladder: (1) <em>Do we need it at all?</em> Could a user study or existing data answer the question? (2) <em>Can it stay on the device?</em> Compute the measure locally and send only the result. (3) <em>Can it be aggregated or noised?</em> Send counts, not events, and use privacy-preserving techniques so that individual behavior cannot be recovered. (4) <em>If it must be collected,</em> collect the minimum, with consent, a short retention period, restricted access and a plan for deletion. &quot;The arithmetic helps decide how much is really needed. To detect a one-point change in completion from a 50% baseline with 95% confidence and 80% power, an experiment needs about 2 × (1.96 + 0.84)² × 0.25 ÷ 0.01² = 39,200 users per group. If only 20% of eligible users opt in to share aggregate data, that's about 196,000 eligible users per group, which is small for a feature used by millions. So we can answer the question with aggregated, opt-in measurement; we do not need detailed event logs from everyone. &quot;The decision: approve a narrowly scoped, aggregated, opt-in measurement with a two-year review, and decline the raw event log. What would change my mind: a quality problem that aggregation can't diagnose (for example, rare failures), in which case I'd propose a time-limited, consented diagnostic program for a small group, with deletion at the end.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> A decision question; a ladder of less intrusive options; sample-size reasoning; a decision with conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you decide what to leave out of a new feature?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I cut anything that's not essential.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Anchors on the user's core job and removes what does not serve it.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Defines the job, tests each element by removing it, uses usage evidence, protects against hidden costs of complexity, and handles the pressure to add options and settings.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I start with the job the feature does for the person, in one sentence: for example, 'set up a new device in under two minutes without reading anything.' Then each element has to earn its place: if I remove it, does the job still get done? I'd test that in a prototype with users: take away one thing at a time and watch for confusion. Suppose a settings screen has 12 options, and usage data show the top three account for 91% of changes. The other nine can be defaults with sensible behavior, or moved into an advanced area, because each visible option has a cost: it must be understood, tested, translated and supported, and it makes the common path less clear. &quot;I'd also ask what the omitted thing costs the people who need it: for some options (accessibility settings, say), the usage share is low and the importance is high, so usage alone is not the criterion. I'd keep those and make them easy to find. When stakeholders ask for more options, I'd ask what problem each solves and whether a better default solves it without a choice. And I'd write down what we left out and why, so that the decision can be revisited with evidence instead of being relitigated by whoever shouts loudest.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> The job as the test; removal tests; evidence plus judgment; protection of high-importance, low-usage needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you prioritize accessibility features against features for the mainstream?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Accessibility affects fewer users, so mainstream features come first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats accessibility as a requirement and a benefit for all.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Rejects the framing as a trade-off, sizes the need, uses the benefit to everyone, sets a floor, involves users with disabilities, measures it, and handles cost honestly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd challenge the framing. Accessibility isn't a competitor to mainstream features; it is a quality bar for every feature, and some accessibility work is also a mainstream feature. A widely cited estimate from the World Health Organization is that about 1.3 billion people, around one in six, experience significant disability <code>Public</code> <code>[V: WHO fact sheet on disability]</code>, so it is not a small segment, and many more have temporary or situational limits (a broken arm, a loud room, bright sun). &quot;So I'd set a floor: a feature does not ship if it fails the accessibility requirements for its type (screen reader support, text scaling, contrast, captions, motor alternatives). That is part of the definition of done (C16.8), not a separate prioritization. Beyond the floor, dedicated accessibility features are prioritized on need, with people with disabilities in the research and the design, not for them. Look for features that serve both: captions help the deaf and anyone in a noisy place; voice control helps people with motor limits and anyone with busy hands. &quot;I'd measure it: the share of features meeting the bar at launch, task success for users of assistive technology in testing, adoption of the features, and satisfaction in that group, with the legal and ethical requirements as a floor. Cost is real, and cheaper at design time than later; I'd say so to stakeholders with an example. I would not defend it as a cost-benefit calculation alone; it is also a matter of who the product is for.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Reframes the question; sizes the need; a floor in the definition of done; co-design; honest about cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How would you improve how families share and organize photos together?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Add more sharing options and albums.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Segments users, picks a problem and proposes a solution with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes roles (parent, child, grandparent), identifies the problem of selection and consent, considers privacy for children, proposes a test, names tradeoffs, and measures contribution and engagement across the group.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd start with who is in a family library: the parent who organizes, the other parent who contributes, a teenager who wants privacy, and a grandparent who just wants to see the grandchildren. Their jobs differ. The organizer wants a complete, tidy record; contributors want sharing to be effortless; the teenager wants control over what is visible; the grandparent wants a simple way to see the best of recent photos without sorting through thousands. &quot;I'd pick the problem that seems largest: relatives drowning in volume and not seeing the moments that matter. Evidence I'd look for: the share of shared photos that are ever viewed by the other members; the share of members who stop opening the library after the first month; and interviews with grandparents. Suppose (invented) only 12% of shared photos are viewed by a second member and a third of non-organizers stop within a month. &quot;The solution: a 'highlights' view for each event that shows the three or four most representative photos (selected by quality and diversity of people and moments), with the full set one tap away, and a weekly digest for members who want it. Tradeoffs: automatic selection can hide a photo someone cares about, so I'd always make the full set available and let anyone flag a missed photo; selection of images of children raises privacy concerns, so processing stays on the device where possible and the organizer controls who sees what. &quot;Metrics: the share of shared photos viewed by a second member (primary), members active at 90 days, and contributions per member; guardrails: complaints, flagged misses and opt-outs. I'd test with a holdout of families for four weeks and read by member role, since the benefit for grandparents may come at a cost to organizers if they feel they lose control.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Roles and jobs; a specific problem with evidence; privacy and consent; role-level measurement.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-09\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-09</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What questions would you ask your interviewers?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;What's the culture like? What do you like about working here?&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tailors questions to the interviewer and role.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Asks questions that show preparation and judgment, listens, uses an answer in a follow-up, and avoids questions answered on the careers page or about pay in early rounds.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd tailor two or three questions to the person. To the hiring manager: 'What would success look like in the first six months, and what is the hardest problem the team is working on where you'd want help?' To a peer PM: 'Tell me about a decision you changed your mind about recently and why.' To an engineer: 'What makes a PM easy to work with from your side, and what makes one hard?' To a senior leader: 'What do you think will matter most for this product in the next two years?' To the recruiter: 'What does the rest of the process look like, and what do you see in the strongest candidates?' &quot;I'd listen and use the answer. If the manager says the hardest problem is onboarding for new users, I'd say, 'In my last role we found the first-day drop-off was in the permission step; is that where you see the loss?' That shows I was listening. I'd avoid questions I can answer from the careers page, questions about pay or vacation with the interviewers (save them for the recruiter), and empty flattery. I'd keep to two or three, so that I don't run over time, and I'd note the answers for the follow-up message.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Tailoring; curiosity; listening; judgment about what to ask whom.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-10\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-10</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> interview-craft · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C33 · <strong>Competency:</strong> IC · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You receive a 48-hour take-home product case. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd spend the 48 hours making it as good as possible.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Restates the question, plans the structure and edits.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Time-boxes to the stated expectation, answers first, includes one or two numbers and a segment insight, states assumptions, recommends with risks and a test plan, and cuts the length.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> &quot;I'd treat the 48 hours as a limit and not a target. If the company says it should take about eight hours, I'd spend about eight. A sprawling submission suggests poor judgment, and the reader's time is limited. &quot;I'd restate the question and the audience in two sentences, then plan the structure: page one has the recommendation and the key number; page two the user, the problem and the evidence; page three the options and tradeoffs; page four the test plan, the decision rule and the risks. I'd budget the time: two hours to think and gather facts, three to write, one for numbers, one to edit, one buffer. &quot;I'd show one or two numbers with the logic, and look for a segment insight that an average would hide. For a subscription proposal, for example, the average member may be profitable while the heaviest users are not. I'd state my assumptions, make a clear recommendation, and say what I would do with more time. Then I'd edit: cut a third, move the answer to the top, check every number, and read the prompt again to make sure I answered what was asked. I'd send it early and keep a record of my assumptions in case there is a follow-up conversation.&quot; <em>Signals.</em> Respect for the time limit; answer first; numbers and a segment insight; editing; a plan to defend it.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c33-11\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C33-11</h3>\n";
